WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.140
Web reveals dust and water forming right next to the Milky Way's central black hole, defying

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:12.880
expectations for such a harsh environment. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 158.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.460
An international team use the james webb space telescope to find dust and water surviving close

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:26.060
to the Milky Way's supermassive black hole. The evolved star, IRS-3, continues releasing

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:31.900
newly formed material, despite intense radiation from the central region. These observations

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:38.100
show material can form and persist in environments previously considered too hostile. The findings

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.300
come from mid and near infrared imaging that isolates the star's contribution from the

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.960
surrounding glow. Web sensitivity allowed astronomers to separate the star's output from

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.920
the bright background of Sagittarius AR itself. Additional spectra will help determine the

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:00.860
exact temperatures and densities where the water molecules remain stable. Now shifting from

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.860
the extreme environment around a black hole to the more familiar but still intricate structures

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:13.100
left by ordinary dying stars. Web captured the planetary nebula in mid-infrared light, highlighting

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:19.360
the complex structure of swept up gas. Lower mass stars like the one that formed the nebula

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.880
shed outer layers instead of exploding as supernovae. The white dwarf at the center is ionizing

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:32.620
an expanding bubble of gas that forms the lion's face and destroys dust in its path. On going

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:39.560
research focuses on how these rings and shells develop in nebulae of this type. The mid-infrared

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:45.400
view isolates the heated dust and ionized gas without visible light confusion from foreground

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:51.740
stars. Follow-up observations will track changes in the bubble's expansion and dust destruction

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:58.460
over time. A typical planetary nebula spans roughly one light year across, about the distance

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:05.200
a commercial airliner travels in 200,000 years of continuous flight. When a sun-like star

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.640
exhausts its core hydrogen, it swells into a red giant, and then ejects its outer envelope

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:17.560
in successive pulses, spaced centuries or millennia apart. The exposed hot core, now a white

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:24.060
dwarf, floods the ejected gas with ultraviolet photons that ionize it and drive a fast wind outward

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:30.040
at hundreds of kilometers per second. That wind sweeps up earlier, slower material into dense

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.980
shells and rings, whose spacing records the timing of each ejection episode. Temperatures

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:41.660
in the ionized zones reach tens of thousands of degrees, while the shells themselves expand

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:47.420
at only 20 to 30 kilometers per second. Slow enough for astronomers to watch structural

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:53.360
changes over decades with repeated imaging. The same central star can produce both intricate,

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:58.360
multi-layered patterns and simple spherical shells depending on the mass-loss history

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:04.520
and any binary companion that may be present. Yet the precise combination of factors that

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:11.200
decides which outcome occurs in any given nebula remains unknown. From the shaping of planetary

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.860
nebulae to another case where Mars' surface history is being rewritten, new analysis challenges

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:24.100
the long-held view that the Martian surface froze solid around 3 billion years ago. Evidence

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:29.400
suggests liquid water could have persisted later than previously modeled, altering timelines

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:36.020
for surface habitability. Researchers examine geological indicators that imply episodic melting

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:42.840
or groundwater activity after the presumed global freeze. Further orbital and rover data will test

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:49.780
whether these conditions lasted into more recent epochs. The revised chronology rests on reexamination

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:56.200
of valley networks and mineral deposits previously dated to earlier periods. Models incorporating

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:02.020
these observations now allow for brief intervals of surface liquid after the main freeze-over.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:09.000
From past water on Mars to the rover now climbing toward a key geological boundary in Gale crater.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:15.500
The rover is approaching a large-scale erosional super-surface in Gale's sedimentary record

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:25.540
that marks a shift from net deposition. Planning for souls 4,968 through 4,974 focuses on

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:31.720
characterizing this feature through targeted imaging and contact science. The super-surface records

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:37.540
a period when environmental conditions change to cross the crater floor. Scientists will compare

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.440
rock textures and chemistry above and below the boundary to understand the transition.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:49.320
Mass cam and chem cam observations will document layering and mineralogy at multiple scales along

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:55.340
the climb. The data will help reconstruct how long the change in depositional regime lasted

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:01.800
and what caused it. From surface exploration on Mars to statistical searches for ice beneath it.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:08.840
Two studies from the Planetary Science Institute now assign a 64% probability of finding ice beneath

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:15.240
specific patches of Martian mid-latitude ground. The work moves beyond simple consistency checks

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:22.100
with orbital data to probabilistic assessments. Targeted digging or drilling at high probability sites

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:27.860
could test the predictions directly. Results will guide landing site selection for future ice

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:34.800
access missions. The probability maps incorporate both neutron spectrometer data and thermal inertia

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:41.120
measurements from orbiters. Sites with the highest scores also show morphological features consistent

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:47.480
with ice-rich material just below the surface. From Mars' subsurface prospects to rare alignments

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:54.620
that let astronomers weigh distant galaxies. A neural network scanned a catalog of 800,000 quasars

00:05:54.620 --> 00:06:01.340
and identified seven cases where the host galaxy lenses a background object. These alignments allow

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:07.300
astronomers to weigh the quasar host galaxies despite the bright central engine drowning out starlight.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:13.380
The method bypasses the usual difficulty of separating quasar glare from galactic mass.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:19.420
Follow-up spectroscopy will confirm the lensing geometry and refine mass estimates.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:24.740
The seven systems represent an extremely small fraction of the input catalog,

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.780
underscoring how rare the required alignments are. Each confirmed lens will provide an independent mass

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:38.200
measurement for its host galaxy at a time when the quasar was actively accruing. From clever data

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:44.620
analysis to an algorithm now scheduling entire telescope nights. The algorithm learned from years of

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:51.140
actual astronomer decisions rather than following predefined scheduling rules. It now selects targets

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:58.040
that maximize usable data given weather, moonlight, and atmospheric conditions. Early tests show the

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:04.740
system reduces wasted time on marginal observations. Continued training on new nights will improve its

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:11.080
ability to anticipate changing conditions. The model was trained on logged observations from multiple

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:17.720
facilities capturing patterns that human schedulers had developed through experience. Real-time updates

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:23.980
allow the system to reprioritize targets when clouds move in or seeing deteriorates unexpectedly.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:29.700
From software optimizing observations to hardware that can be shipped anywhere for launches.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.640
The portable launch system fits all required infrastructure into standard shipping containers for

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:42.020
rapid deployment. Ghost is designed to support neutron and electron operations from new sites with

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:48.300
minimal fixed construction. The approach aims to increase launch flexibility and geographic options.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:54.560
Initial demonstrations will validate transport, setup, and countdown procedures under field conditions.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:08:01.300
Each containerized module contains power, communications, propellant handling, and safety systems needed

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:07.820
for a complete launch campaign. The design allows a launch pad to be established in weeks rather than

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:16.960
the months or years required for traditional fixed facilities. From portable launch capability to a

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:23.220
Canadian operator has reserved multiple flights on rocket labs upcoming medium lift vehicle for optical

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:31.060
relay satellites. The first dedicated mission is scheduled for 2028. Kepler plans to use the launches to

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:38.040
expand its next-generation constellation architecture. Contract details confirm payload capacity and orbital

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:44.420
delivery requirements. The optical relay satellites will provide high bandwidth data links between other

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:50.420
spacecraft and ground stations. Neutron's larger fairing and lift capability compared with

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:55.700
electron will allow Kepler to orbit heavier, more capable platforms in a single launch.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.900
From commercial launch contracts to an astronaut preparing for her first spacewalk,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:10.040
ESA astronaut Sophie Adonaut will join NASA astronaut Anil Menon for US Spacewalk 97 on 18 August.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.100
The pair will replace a space-to-ground antenna on the International Space Station

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:18.680
to maintain high-speed data and voice links with Houston.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:26.620
Coverage begins at 1300 Central European Summer Time with the spacewalk starting around 1435

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:35.000
Central European Summer Time. Live streams will be available on ESA, Web TV, and the ESA YouTube channel.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.900
The antenna swap is required because the current unit has reached the end of its operational life

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:47.060
after years of continuous service. Ground teams have already prepared the replacement hardware and

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:53.900
verified all tools for the extra-vehicular activity. From human operations in orbit to recent rocket

00:09:53.900 --> 00:10:02.680
successes and setbacks on the ground, Japan's H3 launched the 10,800-pound Mitsubiki 7 satellite from

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:09.260
Tainagashima on 10 August. The spacecraft will support regional navigation services from a high

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:16.020
Earth orbit. The mission marked another operational flight for the H3 vehicle following earlier tests

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:22.120
and demonstration launches. Ground teams confirmed successful separation and initial acquisition.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:29.400
Mitsubiki 7 joins an existing constellation that augments GPS coverage over Japan and surrounding

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:36.200
waters. The satellite's mass required the full performance of the H3's enhanced upper stage for

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:42.540
the transfer to its final orbit. From a smooth Japanese launch to a Chinese failure the same week.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:48.700
The rocket broke apart less than 90 seconds into flight from Wenchang carrying a rumored military

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:54.440
communications satellite. Public footage captured the explosion during first stage ascent.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:59.800
Chinese state media have not yet released an official statement or failure analysis.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:06.260
Investigations will examine telemetry to determine the cause. The failure occurred while the

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:12.440
first stage was still burning before any planned staging events. No injuries or ground damage have

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:18.300
been reported from the launch site. From launch outcomes to new ways of sharing space content with

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:26.000
the public. Continuous NASA content is now available on Discovery Plus live channels with special events

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:33.600
including Artemis III also streaming on HBO Max. All broadcasts remain freely accessible on NASA

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:40.480
Plus and social platforms. The expanded distribution aims to reach broader audiences beyond traditional

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:46.400
viewers. Program schedules will continue to include mission coverage and educational segments.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.620
The partnership brings live mission commentary and documentary programming to platforms already

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:59.300
used by millions of households. NASA Plus will retain the complete on-demand archive and real-time

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:05.280
mission feeds without subscription requirements. From audience reach to opening mission software to

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:11.840
outside coders. The agency invites coders worldwide to contribute and run software on the spacecraft

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:18.680
during its asteroid encounter. Selected experiments will execute in deep space aboard the Hara probe.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:24.420
The initiative expands community participation in mission operations and data analysis.

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:29.620
Proposals will be evaluated for technical feasibility and scientific value.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:36.400
Hara's onboard computer resources allow multiple guest experiments to run alongside the core mission

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:42.520
software. Successful submissions could demonstrate new autonomy algorithms or data processing

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:48.760
techniques in the actual radiation and thermal environment of deep space. From open spacecraft

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:54.860
software to the final weather satellite briefing. Journalists can register for online briefings in

00:12:54.860 --> 00:13:00.540
English, French and German ahead of the media set, third generation imager satellite launch.

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:07.320
Applications close 17 August. The sessions will cover mission objectives, spacecraft capabilities

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:13.840
and launch preparations. The satellite will enhance European weather monitoring from geostationary

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:21.280
orbit. MTG-I-2 carries an advanced imager that provides higher resolution and more frequent observations

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:27.540
than previous media sat generations. The briefings will also address launch vehicle integration

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:33.580
and expected post-launch commissioning timeline. Keep an eye on the first results from those global

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:40.500
developer experiments once Hara reaches its target asteroid. That covers today's space and science

